title: CloudWatch Logs Insights and Cloud Logging queries, hands-on
guide: howto/cloud-native-log-queries.html
promise: Learn both query languages from zero on 24 CloudTrail events, and recognise a query that returns nothing because it cannot work.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
Beginners who can use a terminal should allow about two hours for both halves: CloudWatch Logs Insights in Amazon Web Services (AWS) and Google Cloud's Logs Explorer. Either works alone. The 24 lab events sit well inside free log allowances.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
Every command and query ran as printed on 4 November 2026.

Only the syntax of the Google half's step 8 filters was tested: nothing matched them. Limits and timings the lab data cannot reach come from AWS's and Google's documentation.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Paste BASH blocks into your terminal. Run LOGS INSIGHTS blocks in CloudWatch's Logs Insights. Run CLOUD LOGGING blocks in the Logs Explorer. Each half's last step deletes what you made, bar one AWS metric that costs nothing once unused.

== Set up AWS: a throwaway log group
section: 1. Make a throwaway log group
Use a non-production AWS account that AWS command-line interface version 2 can already reach, and jq. These commands make a log group, a named collection of log events; each prints nothing when it succeeds. Step 2 loads the lab data.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/log-queries-lab && cd ~/log-queries-lab
aws logs create-log-group --log-group-name csoh-lab-cloudtrail
aws logs put-retention-policy --log-group-name csoh-lab-cloudtrail --retention-in-days 1
aws logs create-log-stream --log-group-name csoh-lab-cloudtrail --log-stream-name lab
```

== Set up Google Cloud: a throwaway log
section: 1. Write the lab to a throwaway log
Use a non-production Google Cloud project the gcloud command-line tool already reaches. Check that the second line prints the project you mean; the loop writes each line of cloudtrail-lab.jsonl, which the AWS half's step 2 saves, as an entry.
```bash
cd ~/log-queries-lab
gcloud config get project
while IFS= read -r event; do gcloud logging write cloudtrail-lab "$event" --payload-type=json; done < cloudtrail-lab.jsonl
```

== Two log stores, two query languages
section: Two log stores, two query languages
Logs Insights reads AWS's CloudWatch Logs; the Logs Explorer reads Google's Cloud Logging. A Logs Insights query is a pipeline: commands joined by pipes, |, that filter and count. A Cloud Logging query is a true-or-false filter that cannot count.

== The lab: 24 CloudTrail events and a leaked key
section: The lab: one leaked key, five detections
The lab is 24 CloudTrail events from one morning. CloudTrail is AWS's record of the calls made in an account.

The access key of build-bot, a continuous integration (CI) user, has leaked.

== What someone does with build-bot's key
section: The lab: one leaked key, five detections
With the key, from 203.0.113.50, someone is mostly refused, then creates the user backup-svc, attaches AdministratorAccess (the administrator policy), gives it an access key and uses it to stop CloudTrail logging.

Around that, bob signs in without multi-factor authentication (MFA).

== include: _cloudtrail-lab.deck

== Nothing found, or looked in the wrong place?
section: Where a query looks
Both languages read only the logs and hours chosen outside the query; a wrong choice silently returns nothing. Run at Last 1 hour, this Cloud Logging query finds StopLogging, the call stopping CloudTrail logging. Event fields sit under jsonPayload.
```cloud logging
jsonPayload.eventName="StopLogging"
```

== Count what arrived before you filter
section: 3. Run a query, and look inside first
In the CloudWatch console's Logs Insights, select csoh-lab-cloudtrail and a time range including your load. A load can be refused without an error, so run this query first. Expect events 24 and parsed 24, the events with an eventName field.
```logs insights
stats count(*) as events, count(eventName) as parsed
```

== Before you trust an empty result, run a control
section: 6. Count, then threshold: the burst that never fires (D2)
A Logs Insights D2 draft, alerting at five AccessDenied errors per address, returns nothing: the attacker has four. This control counts codes: expect AccessDenied 7, four of them the attacker's, and Client.UnauthorizedOperation 3, the attacker's Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) refusals.
```logs insights
filter ispresent(errorCode)
| stats count(*) as events by errorCode
```

== The Logs Explorer shows the last five minutes
section: 2. Find what arrived
In the Google Cloud console, open Logging, then Logs Explorer. Set the time range to Last 1 hour, not the default, and run this Cloud Logging query; expect 24 entries. The log_id() function picks a log by its short name.
```cloud logging
log_id("cloudtrail-lab")
```

== In Cloud Logging, OR groups before AND
section: 4. Nested fields, and the precedence trap (D4)
Meant to find StopLogging or an administrator policy attachment, this Cloud Logging query returns only the attachment. It is read as (StopLogging or AttachUserPolicy) and a policyArn containing AdministratorAccess. Use parentheses wherever AND and OR meet.
```cloud logging
log_id("cloudtrail-lab")
jsonPayload.eventName="StopLogging" OR jsonPayload.eventName="AttachUserPolicy" AND jsonPayload.requestParameters.policyArn:"AdministratorAccess"
```

== A log-based alert, tested both ways
section: 7. Make it a log-based alert, test it both ways, clean up
Create the Google half's step 7 alerting policy for D1; it alerts when a new entry matches. Paste these into a terminal minutes apart: only the second should fire. Expect an alert naming backup-svc on Cloud Monitoring's Alerting page.
```bash
gcloud logging write cloudtrail-lab "$(grep -F GetCallerIdentity cloudtrail-lab.jsonl)" --payload-type=json
gcloud logging write cloudtrail-lab "$(grep -F StopLogging cloudtrail-lab.jsonl)" --payload-type=json
```

== Exercise: one address, more than one identity
section: Hands-on exercises
Both halves build D1 to D4; the guide's Exercise 1 builds D5. Which source address used more than one identity? Answer in Logs Insights; say why the Cloud Logging query language cannot. It assumes the lab log group still exists.

== Answer: count distinct identities per address
section: Hands-on exercises
In Logs Insights, expect 203.0.113.50 with two identities: build-bot's leaked key, then backup-svc, the user it created. Count userIdentity.arn, since roles have no user name. A Cloud Logging query decides entry by entry, so it cannot see two at once.
```logs insights
stats countDistinct(userIdentity.arn) as identities, values(userIdentity.userName) as users by sourceIPAddress
| filter identities > 1
```

== Exercise: 17 entries where three were expected
section: Hands-on exercises
A colleague wants the refusals that are not plain AccessDenied, expects EC2's three, and gets 17 entries from this Cloud Logging query on the 24 lab events alone. Where do the other 14 come from, and what should they write?
```cloud logging
log_id("cloudtrail-lab")
NOT jsonPayload.errorCode="AccessDenied"
```

== Answer: in Cloud Logging, NOT and != differ
section: Hands-on exercises
A comparison on a missing field fails and NOT makes it true, so 14 events without errorCode are kept. Write jsonPayload.errorCode!="AccessDenied", false on a missing field, or this query, where :* tests that the field exists. Both return the three.
```cloud logging
log_id("cloudtrail-lab")
jsonPayload.errorCode:* NOT jsonPayload.errorCode="AccessDenied"
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- An empty result proves nothing until you check where and when the query looked.
- Logs Insights silently returns nothing for a misspelled first command, such as filtr for filter.
- Cloud Logging reads lowercase "or" as a search term, not OR.
